Q45. What is the difference between putIfAbsent() and computeIfAbsent()?

    Both can populate a missing or null-valued mapping, but their behaviour is different.
    Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();
    map.put("A", null);
    map.putIfAbsent("A", "Java");
    map.computeIfAbsent("B", key -> "Spring");

    Comparison:
    Feature	putIfAbsent()	computeIfAbsent()
    Existing non-null value	Keeps existing	Keeps existing
    Existing null value	Inserts supplied value	Executes function
    Missing key	Inserts supplied value	Executes function
    Supports lazy calculation	No	Yes
    Function returns null	Not applicable	Does not create mapping

    Real-world use case: computeIfAbsent() is useful for initializing a collection for each key.
    Map<String, List<String>> map = new HashMap<>();

    map.computeIfAbsent("Java", k -> new ArrayList<>())
       .add("Spring Boot");
    map.computeIfAbsent("Java", k -> new ArrayList<>())
       .add("Hibernate");
    System.out.println(map);

    Output:
    {Java=[Spring Boot, Hibernate]}

Q46. What happens when replaceAll() returns null?
    HashMap allows null values, so replaceAll() can replace existing values with null.
    Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();
    map.put("A", "Java");
    map.put("B", "Spring");
    map.replaceAll((key, value) -> null);
    System.out.println(map);

    Output:
    {A=null, B=null}

    Both keys remain present. Only their values change.

Q47.putIfAbsent vs computeIfAbsent
    putIfAbsent is also atomic, but eager.
    The value is created before the call, even if the key already exists.
    computeIfAbsent only creates it when needed.

Q47. Are computeIfAbsent() and compute() atomic in ConcurrentHashMap?

    Yes. ConcurrentHashMap provides atomic implementations of these methods.
    For example:
    ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> map =
        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    map.computeIfAbsent("A", key -> {
        System.out.println("Computing value");
        return "Java";
    });


    ConcurrentHashMap coordinates concurrent updates so that the mapping operation is atomic for the relevant key.
    However, avoid long-running or blocking mapping functions because they can interfere with other updates.
    Also remember that ConcurrentHashMap's mapping functions must not return null to create a mapping.

